# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene before. Luke 14 opens in a house. “And it came to pass, as he went into the house of one of the chief Pharisees to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him” (14:1). Jesus speaks there to those who were bidden, to the one who bade him, and to one who sat at meat with him (14:7, 12, 15). What he says there is told as a parable of a great supper (14:16–24). The tower and the war are not part of that scene.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Verse', 'KJV text', 'What to observe'],
    ['Luke 14:1', '“to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him”', 'The meal in the house of a chief Pharisee.'],
    ['Luke 14:25', '“there went great multitudes with him: and he turned, and said unto them”', 'The turn; the hearers are the multitudes.'],
    ['Luke 14:26–27', '“he cannot be my disciple”; “cannot be my disciple”', 'Two sayings, spoken before the comparisons.'],
    ['Luke 14:28', '“For which of you, intending to build a tower”', 'The first comparison, joined by “For.”'],
    ['Luke 14:31', '“Or what king, going to make war against another king”', 'The second comparison, joined by “Or.”'],
    ['Luke 14:33', '“So likewise, whosoever he be of you”', 'The saying that follows, joined by “So likewise.”'],
    ['Luke 15:1', '“Then drew near unto him all the publicans and sinners”', 'What follows the address.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '2.2 The turn. Luke then writes, “And there went great multitudes with him: and he turned, and said unto them” (14:25). The text does not say where this is. It does not say that the house has been left. It says that great multitudes were going with him, that he turned, and that he spoke to them. The hearers are no longer described as guests at a meal.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The sayings that come first. Jesus does not begin with a story. He begins with two sayings: “If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple” (14:26), and “And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple” (14:27). Both end in the same words, “cannot be my disciple.” The tower and the king follow them.'))
A(('call', 'The Crowd and the Turn',
   'No question from a hearer is recorded before the tower. The address begins because great multitudes were going with Jesus, and he turned and spoke (14:25). The first comparison opens with “For” (14:28), which ties it to the two sayings before it. Luke does not say who in the crowd, if anyone, had asked or intended anything.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The words that tie the parts together. The tower begins, “For which of you, intending to build a tower” (14:28). The king begins, “Or what king” (14:31). The saying after them begins, “So likewise, whosoever he be of you” (14:33). The three small words, “For,” “Or” and “So likewise,” are the connections Luke records. The text states that the tower follows from the sayings of 14:26–27, that the king is set beside the tower, and that the saying of 14:33 is drawn from both. It does not state which part of the stories matches which part of the sayings.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The road and the time. Earlier Luke wrote that Jesus “stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem” (9:51), and at 13:22 that “he went through the cities and villages, teaching, and journeying toward Jerusalem.” Luke does not repeat that note at 14:25. After the address he writes, “Then drew near unto him all the publicans and sinners for to hear him” (15:1). The address sits between a meal in a Pharisee’s house and the next scene in chapter 15.'))
A(('p', '2.6 Why Jesus speaks here. Luke gives no statement of why Jesus spoke these words to this crowd. What Luke gives is the multitudes (14:25), the two sayings (14:26–27), the word “For” (14:28), and the saying that closes the address (14:33). The tower and the war are not free-floating sayings about planning in general. They stand in an address to the crowd, and they are read there.'))
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The two comparisons are read here in the order Jesus gives them. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 14:28', '“sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost”', 'The man intending to build a tower counts the cost.'],
    ['Luke 14:29–30', '“began to build, and was not able to finish”', 'The foundation laid, the finish not reached, the mockery.'],
    ['Luke 14:31', '“consulteth whether he be able with ten thousand”', 'The king consults before the war.'],
    ['Luke 14:31', '“him that cometh against him with twenty thousand”', 'The other king comes with the greater number.'],
    ['Luke 14:32', '“while the other is yet a great way off”', 'The time of the sending.'],
    ['Luke 14:32', '“sendeth an ambassage, and desireth conditions of peace”', 'The sending and the request.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.1 The question about the tower (14:28). “For which of you, intending to build a tower, sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost, whether he have sufficient to finish it?” The comparison is put as a question. A man intends to build a tower. He sits down first. He counts the cost. The thing he weighs is “whether he have sufficient to finish it.” The question is addressed to “you.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 What may happen if he does not (14:29–30). “Lest haply, after he hath laid the foundation, and is not able to finish it, all that behold it begin to mock him, Saying, This man began to build, and was not able to finish.” The text gives the sequence of the failure. He lays the foundation. He is not able to finish. All that behold it begin to mock him. They say, “This man began to build, and was not able to finish.” The text gives the mockery as the thing to be avoided, and it does not say what the tower was for, how large it was, or what it cost.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The question about the king (14:31). “Or what king, going to make war against another king, sitteth not down first, and consulteth whether he be able with ten thousand to meet him that cometh against him with twenty thousand?” This comparison is also put as a question, and it also turns on sitting down first. A king is going to make war against another king. He sits down and consults. The thing he weighs is “whether he be able with ten thousand to meet him that cometh against him with twenty thousand.” The king has ten thousand; the one who comes against him has twenty thousand.'))
A(('p', '3.4 What he does if he is not able (14:32). “Or else, while the other is yet a great way off, he sendeth an ambassage, and desireth conditions of peace.” The text turns on “Or else.” The other king is “yet a great way off.” While he is, the first king sends an ambassage and desires conditions of peace. The text does not say what the conditions are, whether peace is made, or what the first king decides if the answer to his counting is yes.'))
A(('p', '3.5 What follows the stories. Luke 14:33 begins “So likewise.” It is not part of either story. It is Jesus’ own saying after them, and it is recorded in Section 4.'))
A(('p', '3.6 What the stories contain. The text names a man intending to build, a tower, a seat taken first, a cost counted, sufficiency to finish, a foundation, those who behold and mock; a king going to make war, another king, a seat taken first, a consulting, ten thousand, twenty thousand, a great way off, an ambassage and conditions of peace. The two stories share one movement: the one who acts sits down first and weighs what he has against what is required.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What the stories leave unsaid. Jesus does not say who the man building is. Jesus does not say what the tower is. Jesus does not say what the foundation is, or what the sufficient amount is. Jesus does not say who those are that behold and mock. Jesus does not say who the king is, or who the other king is. Jesus does not say who the ten thousand are or who the twenty thousand are. Jesus does not say who sends the ambassage, to whom it goes, or what the conditions of peace are. Neither story says how it ends: the tower is not finished or left unfinished in a told ending, and the war is not fought or settled in a told ending.'))

