A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the builder is a certain person, that the tower is a certain work, that the king is a certain ruler, that the other king is a certain power, that the ambassage is a certain message, or that the peace is a certain peace is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.5 What the text does say. The text says that great multitudes went with Jesus; that he turned and spoke; that he said no man can be his disciple who does not hate, does not bear his cross, and does not forsake all that he hath; that a man intending to build a tower sits down first, counts the cost, and weighs whether he has sufficient to finish; that if he lays the foundation and cannot finish, all that behold it begin to mock him; that a king going to make war sits down first and consults whether he can meet with ten thousand one who comes with twenty thousand; and that, while the other is a great way off, he sends an ambassage and desires conditions of peace. These statements are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation of the parts is given for these two comparisons, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparisons themselves. It assigns no identity to any part of the stories.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparisons. Jesus asks which of those before him, intending to build a tower, would not sit down first and count the cost, and what king, going to make war, would not sit down first and consult. In both, the one who acts weighs what he has against what is required before he begins. The meaning is in what the two stories show of that weighing, the mockery that follows an unfinished work, and the sending of an ambassage while there is time.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparisons communicate.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The cost is weighed before the work begins.** The builder “sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost” (14:28), and the king “sitteth not down first, and consulteth” (14:31). In both, the counting comes first.',
    '**Beginning is not finishing.** The builder is mocked because “This man began to build, and was not able to finish” (14:30). A foundation laid does not make a tower.',
    '**An unfinished commitment is exposed.** The mockery comes from “all that behold it” (14:29). What was begun and not finished is seen by others.',
    '**What is on hand is measured against what is required.** The builder asks “whether he have sufficient to finish it” (14:28), and the king asks “whether he be able with ten thousand to meet him that cometh against him with twenty thousand” (14:31).',
    '**Time can run out.** The king sends the ambassage “while the other is yet a great way off” (14:32). The sending has its moment, and the moment is before the other arrives.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Luke names in this scene are the great multitudes who went with Jesus (14:25). They had just heard that no one can be his disciple who does not hate his own family and his own life, does not bear his cross and come after him (14:26–27). They were meant to understand that to follow him is something to be counted before it is undertaken, that to begin and not finish is to be exposed, and that the saying of 14:33 is drawn from the two stories. Luke does not say that any hearer is the builder or the king, and Jesus does not identify either.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the questions. Both stories are asked, “which of you” and “what king,” and both expect the answer that no one would act without counting. The force then falls on the saying that follows, “So likewise” (14:33), which carries the same weighing into what Jesus says about being his disciple. The force is also in the second story, where the weighing ends not in war but in a request for peace.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The cost is counted first. Beginning is not finishing. An unfinished work is seen. What is on hand is measured against what is required. The time to act can pass. These statements arise from the comparisons, and they do not say that the builder, the tower, either king, the armies or the peace represents anyone or anything. The passage does not use the word “kingdom” in 14:25–33, and the comparison is not made into a statement about it here.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The tower and the war stand in Luke 14 after the parables of the meal. They are kept apart from their neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 14:7–14', '“put forth a parable to those which were bidden”', 'The Lowest Seat and Banquet Invitation (Study 27). Guests choosing rooms, the lowest room, and what to do when making a dinner. It is told at the table, to those bidden and to the one who bade, and it is not a part of the address to the multitudes.'],
    ['Luke 14:16–24', '“A certain man made a great supper, and bade many”', 'The Great Banquet (Study 9). A man, a servant, a supper and excuses. It is told at the table and has no counting of cost, no tower and no king.'],
    ['Matt 7:24–27', '“a wise man, which built his house upon a rock”', 'The Wise and Foolish Builders (Study 18). A man who built a house, the rock, the sand, the flood. The builder of Luke 14:28 builds a tower, counts the cost and is not tested by a flood.'],
    ['Luke 13:22–30', '“Strive to enter in at the strait gate”', 'The Narrow Door (Study 34). A door, a master of the house, a shut door, a knocking. It stands earlier in Luke, on the way to Jerusalem, and it has no tower and no war.'],
    ['Matt 10:37–38', '“He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me”', 'Words of Jesus on family and the cross, in another setting and in different words. They are not an explanation of Luke 14:26–27, and Luke 14:26–27 is not softened by them.'],
    ['Luke 14:34–35', '“Salt is good”', 'The next saying in the chapter. It is not part of the two comparisons, and this study does not read it.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The tower and the war are told to great multitudes who went with Jesus (14:25). The Lowest Seat and the Great Banquet are told at a meal in the house of a chief Pharisee on the sabbath day (14:1). The Wise and Foolish Builders is told in Matthew, in a sermon, and in Luke 6 after “And why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?” (6:46). The Narrow Door is told after “Lord, are there few that be saved?” (13:23). A shared word such as “build” or “enter” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The tower and the war have a man intending to build, those who behold and mock, a king, another king, and an ambassage. The Great Banquet has “a certain man,” one servant and three who make excuses. The Wise and Foolish Builders has a wise man and a foolish man who each build a house. The Narrow Door has a master of the house and those who stand without. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The tower and the war move from a sitting down, to a counting, to a possible failure or a sending for peace. The Wise and Foolish Builders moves from a foundation, to a flood, to a house that stands or falls. The Great Banquet moves from a supper and a sending, to excuses, to others brought in. None of those movements is read into another.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the counting of cost belongs to Luke 14:28 and 14:31 only. It is not read into Luke 14:16–24 or the Narrow Door. Second, the excuses of Luke 14:18–20 are not the leaving of Luke 14:26 and 14:33, and the leaving is not read as the excuses. Third, the builder of the tower is not the builder of Matthew 7 or Luke 6, and a foundation is not read as the same thing in both. Fourth, the “few” of Luke 13:23 and the “many” who “seek to enter in” (13:24) are not carried into the multitudes of 14:25.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to great multitudes who went with him (14:25). The address belongs to the earthly ministry in which he journeyed toward Jerusalem (9:51; 13:22), taught in the cities and villages, and was followed by crowds. The words “cannot be my disciple” are spoken to that crowd, in that ministry.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The address of Luke 14 was spoken before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The discipleship Jesus speaks of in Luke 14:26–33 is the following of him during his earthly ministry. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The tower, the war, the cost and the peace are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The passage is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. Sitting down, counting, beginning, finishing, sending and forsaking are actions in a story and in a saying to a crowd, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The words “cannot be my disciple” speak of discipleship in Jesus’ earthly ministry, and they are not turned into a condition of the Body’s standing. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the stories show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of foresight.** The builder and the king sit down first (14:28, 31). A reader learns to weigh a commitment before making it.',
    '**A lesson of finishing.** The builder is mocked for beginning and not finishing (14:30). A reader learns that a thing begun is a thing to be carried through.',
    '**A lesson of honesty.** The king asks “whether he be able” (14:31). A reader learns to take stock of what he has and not of what he wishes he had.',
    '**A lesson of timeliness.** The ambassage is sent “while the other is yet a great way off” (14:32). A reader learns that some choices have a time, and that the time is before it is too late.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The call to forsake all in 14:33 is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body, and the words “cannot be my disciple” are not turned into a test of the Body’s standing. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this passage.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Great multitudes went with Jesus, and he turned and said that no man can be his disciple who does not hate his own family and his own life, and who does not bear his cross and come after him. He then asks which of them, intending to build a tower, would not sit down first and count the cost, lest after the foundation he is not able to finish and all that behold it mock him. He asks what king, going to make war against another king, would not sit down first and consult whether he can meet twenty thousand with ten thousand, or else send an ambassage while the other is a great way off. Jesus then says, “So likewise, whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple.” He gives no explanation of the builder, the tower, either king or the peace. The comparisons say that the cost is counted first, that beginning is not finishing, and that the time to act can pass. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the builder, the tower, the kings or the peace are, or that makes counting, forsaking or following a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** the multitudes go with Jesus; he turns and speaks two sayings; a man intending to build a tower counts the cost; a king going to make war consults and, if he cannot meet the other, sends an ambassage; Jesus closes with “So likewise.”',
    '**The meaning:** the cost is counted first; beginning is not finishing; what is on hand is measured against what is required; the time to act can pass.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation of the parts from Jesus; no identity for the builder, the tower, the kings, the armies or the peace; nothing carried in from Luke 14’s meal, the builders of Matthew 7 or the Narrow Door.',
    '**The present lesson:** foresight, finishing, honesty and timeliness, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

