A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Lowest Seat and Banquet Invitation, the Great Banquet, the Wise and Foolish Builders and the Narrow Door are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This address makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Great multitudes went with Jesus, and he turned and told them that no man can be his disciple who does not hate his own family and his own life, bear his cross, and come after him. He asks which of them, intending to build a tower, would not first sit down and count the cost, lest he lay the foundation, not be able to finish, and be mocked. He asks what king, going to make war, would not first consult whether ten thousand can meet twenty thousand, or else send an ambassage and ask for peace while the other is a great way off. He then says, “So likewise, whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple.” He names none of the parts of the stories. The comparisons say that the cost is counted first, that beginning is not finishing, and that the time to act can pass.'))
